From 4da9b9f75e3c2ae427fe4cc5df4a8420656721e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Konstantin Pereiaslov <perk11@perk11.info>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 06:29:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/12] wasm: Asyncify-safe futex/condvar + skip the per-insn
 icount2 helper

Two things an emscripten/Asyncify build needs; both are no-ops on every
other platform (__EMSCRIPTEN__-gated).

1) Correct cross-worker condvar wakes.

Under Asyncify, emscripten's pthread_cond_signal/broadcast between web
workers is only delivered when the waiter times out, so a QemuCond used
for cross-thread wakeup (e.g. the pmb887x DSP worker boot-command
handshake) can sleep forever: observed as the L1 task timing out and
the phone never drawing its splash screen.  The engine-level futex
(Atomics.wait/notify) does not go through the Asyncify machinery and
wakes reliably.

  * include/qemu/futex.h: qemu_futex_* on emscripten_futex_wait/wake;
    HAVE_FUTEX becomes defined on emscripten, switching QemuEvent and
    LockCnt to their futex fast paths.
  * util/qemu-thread-posix.c + include/qemu/thread-posix.h: a
    seq+futex QemuCond for emscripten (pthread_cond everywhere else).
    qemu_cond_timedwait now reports real timeouts: qemu_sem_timedwait
    and the thread-pool idle exit actually time out on wasm, like on
    POSIX.

(Isolation note: with the later futex-based main-loop wait in place the
S75 boot no longer deadlocks without this - the broken wake path is no
longer on the critical path - but correct condvar semantics are still
required by any other cross-worker QemuCond user (RCU, thread pool,
device workers), so this stays.)

2) Do not pay the icount2 cycle helper per guest instruction.

TCI helper calls route through libffi, which on wasm is a JS roundtrip
(~us each): a helper per guest instruction capped the interpreter at
~0.6M insns/s.  On emscripten the translator skips the per-insn
gen_helper_cycle_counter (target/arm/tcg/translate.c); the replacement
per-TB accounting is the tci_tbhdr TB-header op of the later
"wasm: tci tb chaining" patch.

Also icount2's 1 MHz frequency floor is relaxed to 1 kHz on emscripten
only (system/icount2.c) - a slow-host guard for the opt-in
precise-clocks mode: on a fast host the controller converges to 3-17
MHz and the floor never binds, but on a phone that sustains <1 MHz the
stock floor makes virtual time run ahead of executed instructions
(deadlines arrive with a fraction of the expected instruction budget).
Measured under 16x CPU starvation (sustained ~2.5 kHz): with the 1 kHz
floor the virtual clock tracks reality (v=377 after 420 s, idle-bias
jumps included); with the stock floor the frequency pins at 1.000 MHz
and virtual time is frozen (v=0.88 after 420 s) - the guest never sees
its timers.  The default timing model (stock -icount) has no frequency
controller and is unaffected.

Also -sASYNCIFY_REMOVE=tcg_qemu_tb_exec (configs/meson/emscripten.txt):
the interpreter is reachable from the asyncify import (libffi's
ffi_call_js), would run fully instrumented (~2x slower) and never
suspends itself - drop it from instrumentation.

ui/wasm.c: wasm_tb_account()/wasm_tbs()/wasm_insns() - per-TB
execution counters the benchmark tooling reads as the throughput
metric; fed from the TCI TB header op.
---
